WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.670
- Well, like Kristin, you
have also been on Broadway.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.580
So it was actually the first
time "Death of a Salesman"

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.290
had an all African American cast, right?

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.000
- Yes, yes, I mean

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.560
- [Kelly] Did that feel like pressure?

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.640
- It felt like a lot of pressure.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.380
I mean, this is a play,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.320
it's one of the greatest American plays

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.120
ever to be written.
- [Kristin] Ever.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:18.953
And your role.

00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:20.790
- And my role as Biff is something

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:24.390
that a young actor just
dreams about being able to do,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.220
let alone on Broadway.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.440
So, when we were gonna do the play,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.640
first, you have to understand

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.610
this play has been done so many times

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.480
- 'Cause it's classic and great.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.313
- It's classic and great.

00:00:34.313 --> 00:00:36.510
So you've either read it,
you've either seen it,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.190
you have either done it in class,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.170
so you have your own
perspective on it, right?

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.250
So how do you do it new?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.710
How do you change someone's
perspective on something

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.690
that's been done so well so many times?

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.790
And then on top of that, it's being done

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.930
with an all Black cast.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.227
So then you have to have the question of

00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:53.220
is it going to resonate?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.290
Will the language read the same way?

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.420
So, there was that pressure,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.780
but I think what we all did with our show,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.790
we didn't change any of the dialogue.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.670
The script stayed the same,
but the nuances changed.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.503
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:06.503 --> 00:01:07.336
- You understand?
- Yes.

00:01:07.336 --> 00:01:09.750
- So we all dug in.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.430
We gave it everything that we had.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.050
We gave our hearts, we gave our spirits,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.855
and that's all you can ask someone to do

00:01:14.855 --> 00:01:16.064
when they're on that stage.

00:01:16.064 --> 00:01:18.285
- Yeah.
- [Kristin] Yes.

00:01:18.285 --> 00:01:21.150
- And young Black actors,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.030
now, they see that they
can broaden the scope

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.560
on the kind of work that they can do

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.550
and that they're able to do.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.800
As long as the story, I believe,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.200
speaks to our humanity on a deep level,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.640
we'll all be able to understand it

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.530
no matter who's telling the story.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.910
- Absolutely.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:36.743
Nice.

00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:38.063
I know, I'm like, gosh.
- So well spoken

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:39.090
and you're right.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.370
- Well, you also did I hear
that you, oh, I got you, girl.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.840
I cry all the time on
this show, check it out.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.673
- Sorry.
- Yep, yep.

00:01:43.673 --> 00:01:44.700
They got a whole load of 'em

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.901
over here for me.
- It's just so well said.

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:47.051
- [Kelly] I know, know.

